WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.520
DAST helps teams evaluate the runtime security behavior of web applications and APIs from an external perspective.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:17.700
It interacts with deployed behavior, so it can reveal issues created by configuration, routing, authentication, session handling, headers, errors, transport, and integrations.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:25.280
It is especially valuable because it observes what the application actually exposes, not only what the source code appears to say.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:39.340
Strong DAST programs depend on authorization, scope, safe environments, useful coverage, authenticated testing, careful active checks, evidence-based triage, remediation validation, and integration into the secure SDLC.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.560
Coverage and context are central.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:48.100
A scan can only speak for the paths, roles, endpoints, and behaviors it reached and exercised.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:56.080
DAST complements secure design, SAST, SCA, code review, manual testing, monitoring, and incident response.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.340
Static analysis can catch risky code before runtime.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:01.880
DAST can observe runtime behavior.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.660
Manual review can reason about business logic.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:07.680
Monitoring and response help after release.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:12.940
Together, these practices provide stronger assurance than any single testing method alone.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.240
The goal is not to run more scans.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:20.100
The goal is actionable evidence that helps teams reduce application risk over time.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:33.040
A durable DAST program protects authorization boundaries, coordinates safely with owners, validates fixes, manages exceptions, and uses recurring findings to improve design, development, testing, and operations.
